WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Airbags were engineered to protect unbelted adults in frontal crashes, yet the same deployment

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.220
force killed children in otherwise survivable low-speed accidents.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.680
This is unintended consequences, episode 116.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.940
In the spring of 1996, a six-year-old girl sat in the front passenger seat of a family

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.380
sedan on a routine errand in Pennsylvania.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.580
When the car struck a parked vehicle at roughly 20 miles per hour, the passenger airbag deployed

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.940
as designed. The bag struck the child with enough force to fracture her skull and neck,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.200
injuries that proved fatal even though the crash itself left the vehicle largely intact.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.600
Regulators and engineers had spent years perfecting a system meant to reduce deaths.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.160
For the smallest passengers, that same system introduced a new and unexpected risk.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.560
The deployment sequence begins when sensors detect a sudden deceleration

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.620
and trigger a chemical reaction that fills the bag in roughly 30 to 50 milliseconds.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.340
That window was calculated to position the cushion before an adult torso can travel forward

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.000
and strike the steering wheel or instrument panel.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.280
For a child whose head sits closer to that panel,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.760
the same rapid expansion leaves little margin for the body to move out of the way.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.780
Federal regulators at the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration began pushing for airbags

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:28.380
in the 1970s and 1980s. Seat belts alone were not being used consistently enough to prevent

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.060
thousands of annual fatalities. The goal was a passive restraint that would activate automatically

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:39.460
in a frontal collision and absorb energy before an occupant struck the steering wheel or dashboard.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.160
Engineers calibrated early designs around the body mass and seating position of an average unbelted

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.260
adult male. That demographic was then responsible for the largest share of crash deaths.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.780
At the time, this seemed a rational engineering choice grounded in the crash data available.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:03.580
The 1991 legislation that required dual airbags in all new passenger cars reflected broad

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.580
agreement that the technology could save lives once it reached the fleet. No one designing

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.060
the systems in the 1980s anticipated that the same energy needed to protect a 170-pound

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.800
adult would later interact differently with much smaller bodies. The choice of a single reference

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.840
occupant also reflected the practical limits of early crash test facilities. Instrumented dummies

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.040
represented only the most common injury patterns observed in hospital records and police reports.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:37.780
By the 1994 model year, most new cars sold in the United States carried driver and passenger

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:44.160
airbags as standard equipment. Manufacturers advertise the devices as an added layer of protection

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.980
that worked even when occupants forgot to buckle up. Early fleet data showed measurable reductions

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.960
in driver fatalities in moderate to severe frontal crashes. Those results reinforce the decision

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:01.640
to proceed with full deployment force. A small number of pediatricians and crash researchers raised

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.060
questions about child passengers. Yet these concerns remained secondary to the larger public

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:13.160
health priority of adult protection. Regulators required prominent warning labels on sun visors,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.120
but the underlying calibration standard stayed unchanged. The system rolled out nationwide without

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.200
a separate performance requirement for occupants under a certain height or weight. Because the

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.640
federal standard measured performance only against the adult male dummy, manufacturers had little

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.040
regulatory incentive to run additional tests with smaller dummies. Production lines therefore

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.060
for installed the same inflator modules

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.920
and vent hole patterns across entire platforms.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.000
Any variation in passenger size was treated

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.760
as outside the design envelope.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.040
Once the airbag equipped fleet grew large enough,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.620
a pattern emerged in real-world crashes

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.100
that had not appeared in the original test dummies.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.380
In low-speed frontal impacts

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.820
that would normally produce only minor injuries,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.540
the rapid inflation of the passenger airbag

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.900
sometimes struck children.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.420
Those children were either unbelted,

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.680
seeded too close to the dashboard, or using a rear-facing infant seed.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.640
The bag's deployment speed and volume, sized for an adult torso, delivered concentrated

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.480
force to a child's head and neck.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.760
By the mid-1990s, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration had begun receiving

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.380
reports of child deaths that occurred in crashes previously considered survivable.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.040
The causal chain traced directly to the design assumption.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:38.700
The same stored energy required to restrain a larger body became excessive for smaller frames positioned closer to the module.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:47.780
Families who had followed earlier advice to place children in the front for better supervision now faced a new hazard created by the safety device itself.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:55.460
The deaths clustered among children under 12 and shorter adults who seating geometry differed from the calibration standard.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:01.240
Because the technology had already been installed in millions of vehicles, the mismatch between

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:06.520
design and assumption and actual passenger population produced a slow but accumulating

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.660
toll before regulators could respond.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.500
One objection sometimes raised is that proper seat belt use would have prevented most of these

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:14.860
cases.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.980
Yet the very reason airbags were added was that large numbers of front seat passengers, including

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.980
children were not consistently belted at the time the standards were written.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:31.200
The same fixed force inflators also posed risks to short-statured adults, whose chests

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.600
sat lower relative to the steering wheel.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:39.220
That overlap further illustrated how a single calibration point left entire segments of

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.360
the population outside the tested range.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.860
In 1997, the agency permitted manufacturers to install deep-powered airbags that deployed

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:54.660
with roughly 20% to 35% less force. Later rule changes introduced multi-stage smart airbags

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:00.540
that used seat weight sensors and crash severity data to tailor deployment. Public messaging

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.500
shifted sharply toward placing all children under 13 in the rear seat whenever possible.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:11.320
These adjustments reduced the incidence of airbag-related child fatalities while preserving most of

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:16.800
the adult protection benefit. The episode also prompted ongoing research into occupant

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.440
classification systems that continue to evolve with each new vehicle generation.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.980
No subsequent redesign has eliminated every edge case, yet the original fixed calibration

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:32.940
approach has been replaced by adaptive logic that accounts for a wider range of body sizes.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:39.020
Even so, the transition required years of fleet turnover because vehicles already on the

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.640
road could not be retrofitted with the new sensors and control modules.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:51.960
Design assumptions that appear reasonable when tested against a single reference body can create hidden hazards for populations that fall outside that reference.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:07:00.800
When a safety system is calibrated to one demographic, its performance for everyone else must be verified with equal rigor rather than assumed.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:06.940
The airbag story also shows that corrective redesign is possible once the mismatch is measured.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.560
That correction can happen only after real-world data reveals the gap.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.620
Today's engineers working on automated emergency braking and occupant monitoring systems face a similar question.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.200
Whose bodies and behaviors are being treated as the default?

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:28.340
And what happens when the default does not match the full range of passengers who will actually use the vehicle?

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.440
We'll leave it there for today.

